WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:08.959
In every small city, there are young men with too much money and too little to do. Monsieur Bamatabois was one of these idle young men.

2
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:16.531
One evening that winter he stood outside a cafe, smoking and watching a poor young woman walk up and down the street.

3
00:00:16.532 --> 00:00:24.110
He was wrapped warmly in a cloak while she wore only a dress. Every time she passed, he called out insults.

4
00:00:24.111 --> 00:00:28.348
"How ugly you are! Do you know you’ve lost your teeth?"

5
00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:34.769
The woman ignored him and continued to pace the sidewalk. Her failure to respond annoyed Bamatabois.

6
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:40.602
So the next time she passed, he grabbed a handful of snow and tossed it down the back of her dress.

7
00:00:40.603 --> 00:00:45.173
"Why, you . . ." The woman swore at Bamatabois and rushed to attack him.

8
00:00:45.174 --> 00:00:55.332
A crowd began to gather. Bamatabois defended himself as the woman scratched his face and kicked his shins. The woman was Fantine.

9
00:00:55.333 --> 00:01:02.165
Suddenly a tall man advanced from the crowd, seized her by the waist, and said, "You’re under arrest for assault."

10
00:01:02.166 --> 00:01:09.324
Fantine looked up and stopped screaming at once. She shuddered with terror because she knew that he was a policeman.

11
00:01:09.325 --> 00:01:14.491
It was Javert. Meanwhile Bamatabois used that moment to slip away.

12
00:01:14.492 --> 00:01:20.499
Javert quickly broke up the crowd and walked toward the police station, dragging Fantine behind him.

13
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:28.165
At the station Javert wrote out the charges against her. When he finished writing, he said to the guard, "Take this woman to jail.

14
00:01:28.166 --> 00:01:34.641
She has attacked an honest citizen." Then, turning to Fantine, he said, "You’re sentenced to six months."

15
00:01:34.642 --> 00:01:42.372
"No!" screamed Fantine. "What will happen to my child? Cosette will be thrown into the street.

16
00:01:42.373 --> 00:01:51.284
I was wrong to attack the gentleman and ruin his hat. Where is he? I want to beg his pardon. Oh, please have pity on me!"

17
00:01:51.285 --> 00:01:55.538
"It’s too late for that," said Javert. "Take her away!"

18
00:01:55.539 --> 00:02:00.284
"Just a moment!" A man had entered the room while Fantine was pleading with Javert.

19
00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:03.721
Javert turned around. "Yes, Mayor Madeleine?"

20
00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:09.443
"So you are the mayor!" hissed Fantine, and she spat in his face.

21
00:02:09.444 --> 00:02:13.768
The mayor wiped his face and said to Javert, "Set this woman free."

22
00:02:13.769 --> 00:02:18.014
Javert could not believe what he was hearing, and neither could Fantine.

23
00:02:18.015 --> 00:02:27.014
"This is the same mayor who had me fired from his factory!" cried Fantine. "And all because he listened to the gossip of old women."

24
00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:32.321
"I can’t let her go," said Javert. "She has insulted you and attacked a citizen."

25
00:02:32.322 --> 00:02:38.646
"I was passing down the street when you arrested her," said the mayor. "I talked to some people in the crowd.

26
00:02:38.647 --> 00:02:43.559
She didn’t do anything wrong. It was Bamatabois who attacked her

27
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:49.472
." Javert was not moved. "I have the authority here, and I say she goes to

28
00:02:49.473 --> 00:02:54.472
jail." "Perhaps you should check the law regarding illegal imprisonment," said

29
00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:58.498
Madeleine. Javert glared at him, but the mayor stared right

30
00:02:58.499 --> 00:03:01.472
back. "Get out!" said the mayor to

31
00:03:01.473 --> 00:03:07.964
Javert. Javert looked startled, but recovered quickly. He bowed to the mayor and left the police

32
00:03:07.965 --> 00:03:13.218
station. Later that night Javert sat down and wrote a letter to the chief of police in

33
00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:20.107
Paris. Meanwhile Fantine had stood by the door and watched Javert leave. She did not know what to

34
00:03:20.108 --> 00:03:26.853
think. The man who had saved her was none other than Mayor Madeleine, the person she blamed for all her

35
00:03:26.854 --> 00:03:31.948
woes. At the very moment when she had insulted him, he had rescued

36
00:03:31.949 --> 00:03:37.504
her. She quickly felt her hatred melting away, replaced by feelings of warmth toward

37
00:03:37.505 --> 00:03:44.575
him. Madeleine turned to her and said, "I had no idea that you had left my factory. Why didn’t you come to me for help?"

38
00:03:44.576 --> 00:03:49.425
"Because the supervisor told me that you were the one who wanted me fired."

39
00:03:49.426 --> 00:03:56.837
"Oh, you poor woman!" Madeleine’s eyes filled with tears. "I’ll have your child brought here, or pay for you to go to her.

40
00:03:56.838 --> 00:04:02.639
You shall live here, or in Paris, or wherever you like. You shall do no more work unless you want to.

41
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:10.139
I’ll give you all the money you need, and I’ll pay all your debts. I’ll be responsible for you and your child from now on."

42
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:17.393
This was more than Fantine could bear. To have Cosette by her side! To live free from debt and worry!

43
00:04:17.394 --> 00:04:21.639
She was unable to say anything to Mayor Madeleine and could only sob.

44
00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:30.306
Her legs gave way, and she sank to her knees before the mayor. She reached out to kiss his hand, and then she fainted.

45
00:04:30.307 --> 00:04:34.226
The mayor had Fantine taken to the infirmary in his house.

46
00:04:34.227 --> 00:04:40.044
A violent fever came over her, and she spent part of the night in delirious ravings.

47
00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:46.718
Finally she fell asleep. When she awoke the next morning, the mayor was praying by her bedside.

48
00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:54.869
That day he wrote to the Thenardiers. Fantine owed them more than one hundred francs; Madeleine sent three hundred.

49
00:04:54.870 --> 00:05:02.956
He told them to bring Cosette to Fantine, who was sick and desperately wanted her child. His letter astonished Monsieur Thenardier.

50
00:05:02.957 --> 00:05:10.686
"Look at this!" Thenardier said to his wife. "Some silly fellow must have fallen in love with Fantine."

51
00:05:10.687 --> 00:05:17.845
Monsieur Thenardier replied to the mayor with a request for more money. He included bills from the doctor and the pharmacy.

52
00:05:17.846 --> 00:05:21.765
The bills were real, but they were for Eponine and Azelma.

53
00:05:21.766 --> 00:05:26.011
Thenardier merely substituted Cosette’s name for theirs.

54
00:05:26.012 --> 00:05:30.424
The mayor immediately sent the money and wrote, "Bring Cosette quickly."

55
00:05:30.425 --> 00:05:37.837
"We aren’t letting go of this girl," said Thenardier to his wife when he read that note. "She’s a gold mine."

56
00:05:37.838 --> 00:05:42.338
Meanwhile Fantine had not recovered and was still in the infirmary.

57
00:05:42.339 --> 00:05:48.741
The mayor visited her twice a day, and each time she asked, "Shall I see Cosette soon?"

58
00:05:48.742 --> 00:05:53.680
He replied, "Perhaps tomorrow. I expect her at any moment."

59
00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.646
And Fantine’s pale face brightened. "Oh, how happy I’ll be!"

60
00:05:58.647 --> 00:06:04.900
But her condition was growing worse from week to week. One day the doctor listened to her lungs.

61
00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:10.304
He shook his head and then took the mayor aside for a private conversation.

62
00:06:10.305 --> 00:06:14.883
"Doesn’t Fantine have a daughter that she’s eager to see?" asked the doctor.

63
00:06:14.884 --> 00:06:17.121
"Yes," replied the mayor.

64
00:06:17.122 --> 00:06:23.463
"Then you’d better get the child here soon because Fantine is going to die."

65
00:06:23.464 --> 00:06:27.316
Madeleine shuddered and went back into Fantine’s room.

66
00:06:27.317 --> 00:06:30.550
"What did the doctor say?" asked Fantine.

67
00:06:30.551 --> 00:06:36.875
The mayor tried to smile. "He told us to bring Cosette at once because that will make you well."

68
00:06:36.876 --> 00:06:44.121
"Oh, he’s right!" she cried. "But what’s the matter with the Thenardiers? Why are they keeping her from me?"

69
00:06:44.122 --> 00:06:47.860
The mayor did not tell her that they were full of excuses.

70
00:06:47.861 --> 00:06:54.764
They said that Cosette was too fragile to travel in the winter, and that they were still adding up the amount of money they had spent on her.

71
00:06:54.765 --> 00:07:01.264
"I will send someone for Cosette," said the mayor to Fantine. "If necessary, I will go myself."

72
00:07:01.265 --> 00:07:04.499
He wrote out a letter for Fantine, which she signed:

73
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:14.969
Dear Monsieur Thenardier: You will deliver Cosette to the bearer of this letter. He will pay all the money that I owe you. Sincerely, Fantine

